      en: "At Manigaon in Tahirpur, on the bank of the Jadukata, more than three thousand silk-cotton (shimul) trees stand in neat rows across roughly 100 bigha — the largest shimul garden in the country. Local accounts say it was planted by a local entrepreneur a little over two decades ago, and it has since become one of the signature sights of any Sunamganj trip.\n\nFrom February to mid-March the trees shed their leaves and burst into scarlet blossom, and fallen flowers carpet the ground in red. Between the rows you glimpse the Meghalaya hills, with the blue Jadukata just beyond — a riot of colour that few places in Bangladesh can match, and a dream for photographers.\n\nAfter the bloom the trees set their cotton pods, and for the rest of the year the garden is a quiet green grove. Spring weekends and holidays get very crowded, so go at dawn or on a weekday if you want the rows to yourself.",
