      en: "Sreemangal in Moulvibazar district is known as the tea capital of Bangladesh. All around town, rolling hillocks are carpeted with clipped green tea bushes under rows of shade trees, as far as the eye can see. Tea has been grown here since British times, and the Bangladesh Tea Research Institute is based in Sreemangal.\n\nWalking or cycling through the estates on a misty morning is the classic experience. Tea pluckers with baskets on their backs, the labour lines inside the estates and the distinctive culture of the tea-garden communities are an essential part of the landscape. A few tea stalls in town serve the famous layered “seven-colour” tea.\n\nOctober to March brings pleasant weather, while the monsoon is when the gardens are greenest and plucking is in full swing. Lawachara National Park, Baikka Beel and Madhabpur Lake are all close by, making Sreemangal a natural base for a few days.",
