      en: "From the border village of Pangthumai in Gowainghat you look straight at a tall waterfall pouring down the face of the Meghalaya hills. The falls themselves lie on the Indian side, but their full height is visible from the Bangladeshi village — a white ribbon of water against green hills that seems to fall straight out of the sky.\n\nThe water collects below in a stream that joins the Piyain system. From the village ghat small boats take you onto the clear water, and a walk along the village paths reveals the quiet rhythms of life in a frontier community. Crowds are thinner than at the big-name spots, so you can enjoy the view in peace.\n\nThe falls are at their fullest in the monsoon (June–September) and shrink considerably in the dry season. Pangthumai pairs easily with Bisnakandi in a single day.",
